# Intro
Two 29.7 Msun stars in a periodic box of molecular gas, with the ISRF transport on
(`ISRF_propagation: 1`) and the H2 line absorption of the transport on
(`ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1`). A dense slab (density contrast 300 at a uniform H2 ratio)
shadows star A. Star B sits in clear gas on the other side of the slab. A reception slot
spans about 90 degrees, and two lights share a slot only where their directions fall in
the same slot. Where the two lights fall in different slots (the check verifies this and
stops otherwise), they do not interact: the field of the two-star run must equal
the sum of the fields of two runs with one star each. The check tests that, tests the
carried H2 column of each star against the straight ray through the slab, and tests the
column of the two-star run against the one-star columns.

Read the README of `ISRFH2StarLit` for the build, the radiation table and the parameters that
the run holds fixed.

# Run
```
./run.sh
```
The run calls swift four times with one binary: star A alone, star B alone, star A
alone again, and both stars. Each run is kept in its own directory (`only_a`, `only_b`,
`only_a2`, `main`). A star keeps its particle ID in the one-star runs, because the ID seeds the
random frame of the emission pixels. `run.sh` reads the number of reception slots from
`config.h` next to the `swift` executable. `contrast` (default 300), `level`, `gas_density`,
`gas_mass`, `h2_ratio`, `n_steps` and `n_threads` override the defaults. `run.sh` stops swift
after `n_steps` steps (default 6; at 8 a lit particle lies within reach of two
images of one star and the check stops). A snapshot is written every `dt_max` (every second
step at half `dt_max`), 1e-18 after a step end, so each snapshot holds the state after a whole
number of steps. The check takes the number of light hops from `timesteps.txt`.

# Checking
`checkH2Attenuation.py twosource` reads the first and last snapshot of the two-star run,
the last snapshots of the three one-star runs (`--only-a`, `--only-b`, `--repeat-a`, run
directories), `fixture.json`, the parameters, the logs and `--slots`. The reference is
snapshot 1, the first written after the first step. The check stops if
the four runs differ in parameters or build, or if the light of the two stars shares a slot
at a particle that holds both.

It tests that:
- the own LW energies of the two-star run equal the sums of the one-star fields, within the
  round-off of a reordered float sum;
- the carried H2 column of each one-star run follows the straight ray from the star through
  the slab and the clear gas;
- where both stars are lit, the column of the two-star run is the mean of the one-star
  columns weighted by their dissociating energies. This needs
  `ISRF_packet_read_mean_time_Myr = 0` (the default).

# Merge leg
```
leg=merge ./run.sh
```
Star B sits behind star A on one line, in uniform gas without the slab. A receiver at
distance d in front of A is at 3 d from B, so the lights of the two stars reach it in one
reception slot and merge there, each with its own H2 column. The run takes three swift calls (`only_a`,
`only_b` and `merge`). `in_line_pc` (d in pc, default 0.2), `star_mass` and `star_mass_b` (Msun,
defaults 10 and 50), `h2_ratio` (default 3e-6, thin enough that the transmission differs between
the two columns), `dt_max` (default 2.5e-14, small so that the H2 stays frozen while the light
arrives) and `n_steps` (default 7) override the defaults.

`checkH2Attenuation.py merge` reads the first and the last snapshot of the `merge` run, the last
snapshots of `only_a` and `only_b`, `fixture.json`, the parameters, the logs and `--slots`. It
takes the gas that all three runs lit, ahead of star A, whose receiving slot holds the whole segment
between the stars, and at most `--max-subtended-deg` (default 10) apart as seen from the receiver. There it tests
that the ratio R of the merged transmission to the transmission of the two lights kept apart
(the one-star transmissions weighted by their energies) lies between 1 and 1 over the energy
fraction of the least-shielded light, and that the merged transmission lies between the
transmissions of the two stars, each within a float term that the check prints. It stops with exit status 4 if
the merge changes the transmission at fewer than 10 receivers, and prints how many receivers lie
beyond the angle limit.
